course: dictionary
lesson: 1284
title: A word with a picture
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A buttercup is a small wild plant with shiny, bright yellow flowers shaped like cups. You can find buttercups in fields and on lawns. «Do not put wild plants or flowers in your mouth; foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» Poisonous means they can make you ill, so never eat them.
explain: Some words also come with a game or a story attached. «Holding a buttercup under a chin to see who likes butter» is a children's game, not a science fact. Ask yourself: is this sentence telling me a fact, or describing a game?
explain: A careful reader checks a claim before believing it. The game says a yellow glow means «you like butter», but the text says «everyone's chin glows, so it proves nothing about liking butter». The clue is the word everyone.
fact: A buttercup is a small wild plant with shiny, bright yellow, cup-shaped flowers.
fact: Buttercups grow in fields and on lawns.
fact: Buttercups are mildly poisonous, so animals avoid them and children should not eat them.
fact: The yellow glow in the chin game happens because a shiny layer in the petals reflects light.
fact: The chin game proves nothing about liking butter, because everyone's chin glows.
exercise: «Do not put wild plants or flowers in your mouth; foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» What does poisonous mean? | Pretty and sweet to smell | *Able to make you ill if you eat or swallow it | Good for making butter | Very tall and thin
exercise: The flowers of a buttercup are shaped like what? | *Cups | Wheels | Bells | Stars
exercise: Lily says the yellow glow under her chin proves she likes butter. What is wrong with her thinking? | *Everyone's chin glows, so the glow proves nothing about butter | Butter makes chins glow, so she is right | Buttercups only glow in the dark | Chins are too small to glow
test: Where might you find buttercups growing? | Inside a shop | *On a lawn or in a field | On the top of a snowy mountain | In the sea
test: Why should children not eat buttercups? | They taste of butter | *They are mildly poisonous | They are too shiny to chew | They are only for animals to eat
exam: «Foxgloves, daffodils and buttercups are poisonous.» What does this sentence tell children to do? | Leave them on the table to dry | Pick them all for a big bunch | Eat them slowly in the sun | *Keep wild flowers out of their mouths
